    "rawCopy": "A Fortune 500 logo on your target account list is a liability if you don't have an economic entry point.\n\nMany enterprise sales teams build target lists consisting entirely of recognizable household brands. Twelve months later:\n- The team has spent 1,000 hours knocking on impenetrable security walls.\n- Zero deals have entered the proposal stage.\n- Quota capacity has been wasted while agile competitors win high-yield mid-market and upper-tier contracts.\n\nHigh-value account prioritization is not an exercise in prestige. It is an exercise in commercial math.\n\nEvaluate your target accounts through three pragmatic filters:\n\n1. Latent Value Yield: Does this organization have multiple operational business units that can expand contract size beyond initial land ARR?\n2. Technical & Process Friction: Will their internal procurement and security review take 14 months, or do they possess modern architectural standards that accelerate deployment?\n3. Verified Economic Accessibility: Can your commercial team identify the P&L owner who is currently held accountable for the specific metric you impact?\n\nIf an account scores 10/10 on revenue size but 2/10 on accessibility and execution readiness, deprioritize it.\n\nFocus your top account executives where value potential and commercial momentum intersect.\n\n#EnterpriseSales #AccountPrioritization #SalesStrategy #B2BGTM #SalesAcceleration",
