    "rawCopy": "A $250,000 enterprise contract looks spectacular on the sales scoreboard.\n\nBut if that customer requires $180,000 a year in dedicated customer success managers, bespoke script maintenance, and cloud compute overages, you didn't win an enterprise asset.\n\nYou won an operational liability.\n\nCost-to-Serve (CTS) is the blind spot of enterprise SaaS finance:\n\n1. Dedicated Personnel Drag: Accounts that demand bi-weekly bespoke business reviews and full-time Slack channel support consume 4x the standard CSM capacity.\n2. Bespoke Technical Debt: Custom connectors written to appease a single customer's legacy database require ongoing engineering maintenance every time your core platform updates.\n3. Compute & Data Ingestion Inefficiencies: Unchecked API querying and unoptimized data storage that inflate your AWS/GCP bills.\n4. Executive Overhead: Senior VP and C-level hours spent defusing avoidable onboarding escalations.\n\nWhen CFOs audit Cost-to-Serve at the account level, they can establish minimum contract thresholds, automate high-frequency workflows, and renegotiate contracts that drain profitability.\n\nDo you calculate the true Cost-to-Serve for each of your top 10 enterprise accounts?\n\n#CostToServe #GrossMargin #EnterpriseSaaS #UnitEconomics #CFO",
