    "rawCopy": "A marketing channel that generates leads for $40 is a financial disaster if those leads convert to revenue at 0.5%.\n\nMost marketing attribution reports focus on cost-per-lead (CPL):\n- The paid social agency boasts: 'We drove 800 leads this month at an average cost of $42!'\n- The marketing team presents a slide full of green metrics.\n- Meanwhile, the sales team complains that none of those 800 leads have a real budget or authority to purchase.\n\nWhen you audit channels on Closed-Won ARR and Cost of Customer Acquisition (CAC):\n- Channel A (Paid Social): $42 CPL -> 0.6% conversion -> $38,000 blended CAC -> 22-month payback.\n- Channel B (Executive Intent & Content): $350 CPL -> 18% conversion -> $11,000 blended CAC -> 4-month payback.\n\nChannel B looks eight times more expensive on paper. In commercial reality, it is more than three times more capital efficient.\n\nHigh-yield revenue leaders manage marketing budgets on Closed-Won Unit Economics:\n1. Stage Progression Yield: How quickly does channel volume advance from Stage 1 to Proposal?\n2. Average Contract Value (ACV) Variance: Do certain channels systematically produce customers that demand heavy discounts?\n3. Gross Margin & Retention by Channel: Do customers acquired through specific campaigns churn at higher rates after year one?\n\nStop managing marketing for lead volume. Steer your demand budget based on cash-flow payback velocity.\n\nWhich of your current acquisition channels is actually generating profitable revenue?\n\n#MarketingEfficiency #CAC #DemandGeneration #RevOps #CFO",
