    "imageExists": true,
    "mdExists": true,
    "rawCopy": "When an enterprise deal slips from June 30 to July 31, reps usually say: 'Legal is just reviewing the redlines. It will definitely close in two weeks.'\n\nTwo months later, the deal is still sitting in the pipeline.\n\nData reveals that 75% of deals that slip once will slip a second time—and over half will ultimately close-lost or go dark.\n\nDeals do not slip because of administrative delays. They slip because of execution gaps earlier in the sales cycle:\n\n1. The Procurement & InfoSec Ambush: The rep waited until the proposal was accepted before asking about security reviews. Procurement suddenly introduces a 6-week vendor vetting process.\n2. The CFO Payback Veto: The business case was pitched around soft productivity gains ('saving our team time') rather than hard cash-flow payback.\n3. Champion Authority Mismatch: The champion loved the platform, but lacked the political clout to push the contract through internal budget committees.\n4. Manufactured Vendor Deadlines: The rep tried to force an end-of-quarter close date without tying it to an agreed customer business event.\n\nStop treating slipped deals as bad luck. Treat deal slippage as a diagnostic signal that your commercial qualification process broke down.\n\nHow many deals currently in your pipeline have pushed their close date more than once?\n\n#DealSlippage #SalesForecasting #RevOps #SalesExecution #PipelineAccuracy",
